---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ComponentPreview from "../../../components/ComponentPreview.astro";
import { tocIconsTree, tocNestedTree, tocPlainTree } from "../../../demos/toc";
import { getLocale, useTranslations } from "../../../i18n";

const t = useTranslations(getLocale(Astro.url));

/* The real Toc.astro renders the site's own rail; it cannot use the tree below because its items
 * are discovered from THIS page's headings at runtime, in the browser, which is what the CLS
 * section further down is about. */
const linkWithIcon = `<a class="sk-toc__link sk-interactive" href="#installation">
  <span class="sk-toc__icon">
    <span data-sk-icon="settings"></span>
  </span>
  <span class="sk-toc__label">Installation</span>
</a>`;

const markup = `<aside class="sk-toc" data-sk-toc>
  <details class="sk-toc__inner" data-sk-toc-disclosure>
    <summary class="sk-toc__summary sk-interactive">
      <h2 class="sk-toc__title">On this page</h2>
      <span class="sk-toc__chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </summary>
    <nav aria-label="On this page">
      <ul class="sk-toc__list"></ul>
    </nav>
  </details>
</aside>`;

const mount = `import { initComponents } from "@skryensya/vanilla/auto";

// Finds [data-sk-toc], wires the rail/disclosure switch and the scroll-spy.
await initComponents();`;

const builder = `<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var toc = document.querySelector("[data-sk-toc]");
    var main = document.querySelector("main");
    var list = toc && toc.querySelector("ul");
    if (!toc || !main || !list) return;

    var headings = main.querySelectorAll(":scope > h2, :scope > h3");
    if (headings.length < 1) {
      toc.setAttribute("data-empty", "");
      toc.setAttribute("data-ready", "");
      return;
    }

    for (var i = 0; i < headings.length; i++) {
      var heading = headings[i];
      if (!heading.id) heading.id = slugify(heading.textContent);

      var item = document.createElement("li");
      item.className = "sk-toc__item";
      item.setAttribute("data-level", heading.tagName.toLowerCase());

      var link = document.createElement("a");
      link.className = "sk-toc__link sk-interactive";
      link.href = "#" + heading.id;
      link.textContent = heading.textContent;

      item.appendChild(link);
      list.appendChild(item);
    }

    if (!window.location.hash) {
      var first = list.querySelector("a");
      if (first) first.setAttribute("aria-current", "true");
    }

    toc.setAttribute("data-ready", "");
  })();
<\/script>`;
---

<Base
  title="Toc"
  description="Toc: the index of a long document. Lists its sections (h2/h3) and marks the current one with scroll-spy."
>
  <h1>Toc</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The index of a document: a list of links to its sections (<code>h2</code>/<code>h3</code>),
    with the current one marked by a scroll-spy. Each row is a link with
    <a href="/en/state-layer"><code>sk-interactive</code></a> (state layer), separated from the
    next by a divider, not by a card. The icon is optional.
  </p>

  <h2 id="anatomia">Anatomy</h2>
  <p>
    A root <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> as the disclosure's shell,
    and inside it a <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> with the list of links.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tocPlainTree(t)} label="Text" flush />

  <h2 id="levels">Levels</h2>
  <p>
    An <code>h3</code> marks <code>data-level="h3"</code> and is indented under its
    <code>h2</code>. The divider stays between rows.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tocNestedTree(t)} label="h2 and h3" flush />

  <h2 id="iconos">Icons</h2>
  <p>
    The link accepts a decorative icon in front of the label — the label already names the
    destination, so the icon carries no accessible name of its own.
  </p>
  <ComponentPreview tree={tocIconsTree(t)} label="With icons" flush />
  <CodeBlock code={linkWithIcon} label="link with icon" />

  <h2 id="rail-o-disclosure">Rail or disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    The contract ships ONE shape: an interactive <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, closed — the
    platform's own behaviour, free. It is a consumer with a column to spare that turns it into an
    always-open, sticky <strong>rail</strong> beside the prose, the same way <code>sidebar</code>
    never decides on its own that it becomes a drawer. On this site, that consumer is the docs
    shell: below <code>wide</code> the same <code>Toc</code> is the <strong>disclosure</strong>
    the contract already ships; from <code>wide</code> up, <code>shell.scss</code> forces it open
    and inert.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> is what makes that switch cheap: the open state, the toggle, the
    keyboard and the accessibility are the platform's. As a disclosure, the rows carry their own
    gutter instead of the container: the tap target reaches both screen edges while the text
    stays on the document's own margin.
  </p>

  <h2 id="espacio">Reserved space</h2>
  <p>
    On this site the grid track is the TOC's width (<code>--docs-toc-inline-size</code>). The
    <code>&lt;aside&gt;</code> is always in flow: before <code>data-ready</code> the list stays
    invisible with a minimum height, so links appearing never push the layout.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={markup} label="HTML" />
  <h3 id="cls">Without CLS</h3>
  <p>
    A tree already knows its items when composed — that is the case above. This site's real shell
    does not: its headings are discovered by walking <code>main</code> in the browser, so an
    <code>is:inline</code> script builds the list before the first paint. With no sections, it
    marks <code>data-empty</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={builder} label="in the layout" lang="html" />
  <p>
    Once mounted, the same enhancer wires the rail/disclosure switch and the scroll-spy that marks
    <code>aria-current</code> for whichever section crosses the top band of the viewport —
    <code>connectToc</code>, behind <code>data-sk-toc</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={mount} label="JavaScript" lang="ts" />

  <h2 id="contrato">Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Root: <code>&lt;aside class="sk-toc" data-sk-toc&gt;</code> with a
      <code>&lt;details class="sk-toc__inner" data-sk-toc-disclosure&gt;</code> inside:
      <code>&lt;summary class="sk-toc__summary"&gt;</code> and
      <code>&lt;ul class="sk-toc__list"&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Shape: one, a closed <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>. A sticky rail is a consumer's decision,
      not the contract's — see above.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each item: <code>sk-toc__item</code> + a bottom divider (except the last). The link is
      <code>sk-toc__link sk-interactive</code>, with its label in <code>sk-toc__label</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Optional icon: <code>&lt;span class="sk-toc__icon"&gt;</code> as the link's first child.
    </li>
    <li>
      Active state: <code>aria-current="true"</code> on the link, written by the scroll-spy at
      runtime.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Base>
